Z||000000||Galen||05-10-2001||11:09 PM||||Whats the advantages of a lighter flywheel? and would it do any good on a rebuilt 88 duke? and where can I get one?<P>------------------<BR>Galen - 1988 Hawaiian Orchid Coupe Auto 4Banger - <A HREF="http://myfiero.spinrpg.com/" TARGET=_blank>My Fiero Website</A><BR>AIM: WookieeNC||||reg||1
Z||000001||MAT88GT||05-11-2001||12:15 AM||||Advantages: slightly faster acceloration<P>Disadvantages: engine will feel as if it has lost some torque<P>Personally I wouldn't get one because of the cost factor, aluminum flywheels are expensive and require replacing a steel friction plate for resurfacing. Not to mention they are somewhat hard to find.<P><P>------------------<BR>1988 Mustang Gt 351w/c4 trans, custom 6pt <BR>1985 Fiero Gt some mods<BR>1986 Fiero 2m4 mint condition||||reg||1
Z||000002||mrfixit58||05-11-2001||07:53 AM||||I believe the Fieroshop has them.<P>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000003||StuGood||05-11-2001||11:48 AM||||I've been reading / thinking about aluminum flywheels a little bit.  Mostly I've been just thinking, so keep that in mind...<P>Aluminum flywheel, if lighter, lowers overall weight of the vehicle (duh), so that's generally a good thing.  Lower rotational inertia says you might get to higher RPMs more a little more quickly (duh 2).<P>But the possible advantage that really appeals to me, is that the aluminum flywheel might be designed to keep the  temperature of the pressure plate assembly (especially, the diaphragm spring) lower.  Heat is what kills my clutch springs - and I hate it when they lose their temper  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">.       ||||reg||3
